You are playing a collaborative grid rearrangement game with a partner.

THE GRID:
- The board is an $GRID_SIZE$×$GRID_SIZE$ grid. Rows are labelled R1–R$GRID_SIZE$ (top to bottom), columns C1–C$GRID_SIZE$ (left to right).
- Cells marked ## are walls — objects cannot enter them.
- Empty cells are shown as a dot (.).

YOUR TASK:
Move all objects to their target positions. The GOAL BOARD shows where each object must end up.
You control ONLY these objects: $YOUR_OBJECTS$. You may ONLY move your own objects.
Your partner controls the remaining objects (shown as X on your board).

RULES:
- On each turn, move exactly ONE object exactly ONE cell: up, down, left, or right.
- Objects CANNOT move into wall cells (##) or outside the grid boundaries.
- Two objects CANNOT occupy the same cell.
- You may only move objects you control.

AVOIDING DEADLOCKS:
- If an object's path is blocked, do NOT keep attempting the same move. Switch to a different object, find an alternative route, or use the REQUEST field to ask your partner to clear the way.
- If you notice you are moving an object back to a cell it just left, you are cycling. Stop immediately and commit to a different approach: reroute via a completely different path, temporarily move another of your objects to open space, or explicitly request your partner to yield.
- "I have no good move" is never a reason to oscillate. A deliberate detour — even several steps out of the way — is always better than bouncing back and forth until the game aborts.

COMMUNICATION:
Before every move you must send your partner a structured message using this exact format:
  PLAN: move [OBJECT] from (Rr,Cc) to (Rr,Cc) — REQUEST: [what you need from your partner, or "none"]

Use coordinates like (R3,C5) to refer to cells. The REQUEST field lets you ask your partner to clear a cell or move out of the way. When deciding your REQUEST, also consider the reverse: is there something YOU could do — a temporary detour, a clearing move — that would unblock your partner without being asked? Cooperation goes both ways.

RESPONSE FORMAT:
You must respond using exactly this format — no other text:
Begin your response directly with `message:` on the very first line — do not output any preamble, thinking, or scratchpad text before it.
message: PLAN: move [OBJECT] from (Rr,Cc) to (Rr,Cc) — REQUEST: [request or "none"]
reason: <your reasoning about what move to make and why>
move: <OBJECT> to R<row>,C<col> (<DIRECTION>)

Example:
message: PLAN: move A from (R2,C3) to (R4,C3) — REQUEST: please clear (R3,C3) if you can
reason: A is at R2,C3 and needs to move down toward its target. The path goes through R3,C3 which may be blocked.
move: A to R3,C3 (down)

DONE OPTION:
Once ALL your objects are at their target positions, you may declare done instead of making a move:
message: done
reason: <explanation>
done: all my objects are placed

Example:
message: done
reason: A is at R3,C5 (target), C is at R6,C2 (target), D is at R1,C7 (target). All placed.
done: all my objects are placed

IMPORTANT: Only declare done if ALL your objects are truly at their targets. An incorrect done declaration ends the game as a failure.

Think carefully before each move. Before choosing your move, reason about both sides: what do you need, and what does your partner likely need? The best move often serves both — and when it can't, be explicit about the tradeoff in your message.
